    "['ip address unnumbered interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces Tunnel10 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces Tunnel10 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.1\\n##  \\n\\n**Explanation:**  \\nIn Juniper, the equivalent of Huawei's `ip address unnumbered interface LoopBack1` is to configure the tunnel interface (`Tunnel10`) to borrow the IP address from the loopback interface (`lo0`). The command explicitly specifies the loopback unit (`lo0.1` in Juniper, analogous to `LoopBack1` in Huawei) under the `family inet` address family.  \\n\\nNote:  \\n- Juniper typically uses `lo0` as the default loopback interface name, and `.1` represents the logical unit (similar to Huawei's `LoopBack1`).  \\n- The `unnumbered-address` directive achieves the same unnumbered IP behavior as Huawei's implementation.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces Tunnel10 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.1'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel-protocol [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface tunnel-interface.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for `tunnel-protocol mpls te`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls interface tunnel-interface.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, MPLS TE tunnels are typically configured under `protocols mpls` with a tunnel interface. The exact implementation might vary slightly depending on the specific Juniper platform and MPLS TE requirements. This is a basic equivalent to enable MPLS TE on a tunnel interface.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface tunnel-interface.0'}]]": 1,
    "['destination [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls static-label-switched-path Tunnel10 to 2.2.2.2', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls static-label-switched-path Tunnel10 to 2.2.2.2\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if this is specifically for the tunnel configuration (as seen in the `interface Tunnel10` context), the Juniper equivalent would be:\\n##\\nset interfaces gr-1/1/0 unit 0 tunnel destination 2.2.2.2\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact Juniper syntax depends on the tunnel type and interface naming. The first translation assumes an MPLS LSP context, while the second assumes a generic tunnel interface configuration. Juniper typically uses `gr-` (Generic Routing Encapsulation) interfaces for tunnels.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls static-label-switched-path Tunnel10 to 2.2.2.2'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces gr-1/1/0 unit 0 tunnel destination 2.2.2.2', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls static-label-switched-path Tunnel10 to 2.2.2.2\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if this is specifically for the tunnel configuration (as seen in the `interface Tunnel10` context), the Juniper equivalent would be:\\n##\\nset interfaces gr-1/1/0 unit 0 tunnel destination 2.2.2.2\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact Juniper syntax depends on the tunnel type and interface naming. The first translation assumes an MPLS LSP context, while the second assumes a generic tunnel interface configuration. Juniper typically uses `gr-` (Generic Routing Encapsulation) interfaces for tunnels.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces gr-1/1/0 unit 0 tunnel destination 2.2.2.2'}]]": 1,
